That's the major reason.

In Lisp, macros are basically compile-time functions (that return code),
and they can call functions that will give you an unused symbol name -
something like #:G443 instead of i; the next call to gensym will give
#:G444, and you're okay so long as you never call your variables
G<number>.

I could probably figure out worse breakages, but that's the one I'm used
to, seeing as people sometimes forget to use gensym in Lisp too.


